# Env
.env
.env.local
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.production.example
# `.env.*` above also swallows any NEW example file. A committed example carries a NAME and no
# value — it is documentation, and it has to be in the repo to be useful to a fresh deployment.
!.env.jev.example
# A NEAR MISS, 2026-10-02: a key was typed into `.env,jev` — a COMMA, not a dot — which matches
# none of the rules above, so `git status` listed a file containing a live credential as
# untracked and ready to commit. `.env*` catches every near-miss spelling (.env,jev, .envjev,
# .env-jev); the negations above keep the committed examples visible.
.env*

